{"name":"First Colours of Spring","hex":"#dbe64c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"first","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Preceding all others of a series or kind; the ordinal of one; earliest.","Most eminent or exalted; most excellent; chief; highest."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Before anything else; firstly.","For the first time."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/first"},{"term":"colours","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of colour"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Flag denoting the nationality of a vessel, flown from the stern."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/colours"},{"term":"spring","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move or burst forth.","To appear."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An act of springing: a leap, a jump.","The season of the year in temperate regions in which temperatures and daylight hours rise, and plants spring from the ground and into bloom and dormant animals spring to life."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spring"}],"entries":[{"title":"Color","blocks":[{"text":"Color is the visual perception produced by the activation of the different types of cone cells in the eye caused by light. Though color is not an inherent property of matter, color perception is related to an object's light absorption, emission, reflection and transmission. For most humans, visible wavelengths of light are the ones perceived in the visible light spectrum, with three types of cone cells (trichromacy). Other animals may have a different number of cone cell types or have eyes sensitive to different wavelengths, such as bees that can distinguish ultraviolet, and thus have a different color sensitivity range. Animal perception of color originates from different light wavelength or spectral sensitivity in cone cell types, which is then processed by the brain."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Colouring_pencils.jpg/330px-Colouring_pencils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color","wikidata":"Q1075"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Peacock_feathers_closeup.jpg/500px-Peacock_feathers_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Peacock_feathers_closeup.jpg/1280px-Peacock_feathers_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Peacock_feathers_closeup.jpg/500px-Peacock_feathers_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The bright colors of peacock feathers are caused by structural coloration","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APeacock_feathers_closeup.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Optimal-color-solid%2CFL4%2CXYZ.gif/250px-Optimal-color-solid%2CFL4%2CXYZ.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Optimal-color-solid%2CFL4%2CXYZ.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Optimal-color-solid%2CFL4%2CXYZ.gif/250px-Optimal-color-solid%2CFL4%2CXYZ.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"MacAdam limits for illuminant CIE F4 in CIE xyY color space","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOptimal-color-solid%2CFL4%2CXYZ.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Munsell_5_PB_5Y.png/500px-Munsell_5_PB_5Y.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Munsell_5_PB_5Y.png/500px-Munsell_5_PB_5Y.png?ut